API key, so others can contribute with scans as well. This is way more resource efficient than everyone running their own scans.\r\n\r\nWhat do you think?","submitter_user":"wofo","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ask","web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jbdtim","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jbdtim/who_wants_crowdsource_llm_scans_articles"},{"short_id":"jnz4v5","created_at":"2026-11-08T06:53:34.868-06:00","title":"A bit about email","url":"https://maurycyz.com/misc/email/","score":17,"flags":0,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"robalex","user_is_author":false,"tags":["email"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jnz4v5","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jnz4v5/bit_about_email"},{"short_id":"sh62d1","created_at":"2026-11-07T17:19:53.437-06:00","title":"I’m not a fan of Deno’s security model (2024)","url":"https://goldstein.lol/posts/deno-security/","score":34,"flags":0,"comment_count":11,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"ThinkChaos","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/sh62d1","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/sh62d1/i_m_not_fan_deno_s_security_model_2024"},{"short_id":"rv1icj","created_at":"2026-11-08T06:07:39.271-06:00","title":"NetBSD 11 desktop on a Raspberry Pi 4","url":"https://neongd.com/articles/netbsd11-rpi4.gmi","score":12,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"jaypatelani","user_is_author":false,"tags":["netbsd"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/rv1icj","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/rv1icj/netbsd_11_desktop_on_raspberry_pi_4"},{"short_id":"5cavvx","created_at":"2026-11-08T03:09:24.240-06:00","title":"Introducing Project Steadfast","url":"https://noteflakes.com/articles/2026-11-07-introducing-project-steadfast","score":7,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"noteflakes","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ruby"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/5cavvx","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/5cavvx/introducing_project_steadfast"},{"short_id":"lh0qbr","created_at":"2026-11-08T02:41:38.183-06:00","title":"Nspawn 3.0: A Docker-compatible Engine API for systemd-nspawn containers","url":"https://blog.nspawn.org/posts/nspawn-3-0-a-docker-compatible-engine-api-for-systemd-nspawn-containers/","score":9,"flags":8,"comment_count":4,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"diktomat","user_is_author":false,"tags":["release"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lh0qbr","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lh0qbr/nspawn_3_0_docker_compatible_engine_api"},{"short_id":"ipplhe","created_at":"2026-11-07T03:47:24.713-06:00","title":"How are you dealing with all the negative side effects of AI on the web?","url":"","score":85,"flags":5,"comment_count":75,"description":"\u003cp\u003eIt seems to me that the AI hype killed the web for good, but not for the reasons we immediately associate with it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSearch engines now rank links full of slop at the top, which ironically make LLM summaries acceptable, not because they are better than what we had before (they are not), but because they at least mention the good links which are now buried at the bottom of the page. Also, they've been rate limiting ordinary users like crazy: more often than I'd like to, Google and DuckDuckGo now either show me either an error message or tell me to wait a bit. Sometimes I waste at least a minute refreshing the page and waiting for a result.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI also can't browse GitHub freely without being logged in. Anubis is now everywhere, occasionally blocking me. I feel like my productivity has actually gone down thanks to all of that.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eEdit: I see that most of the replies here are suggesting relying less on the web and going after more fundamental resources, which are great suggestions, don’t get me wrong! But a book from 5 years ago will not help you understand why your custom firmware doesn’t boot in your 1-year old mobile chipset. A existing bug report on the web probably will. Not quite my predicament at the moment, but something very similar for which the web seemed to work just fine before the AI craze.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"It seems to me that the AI hype killed the web for good, but not for the reasons we immediately associate with it.\r\n\r\nSearch engines now rank links full of slop at the top, which ironically make LLM summaries acceptable, not because they are better than what we had before (they are not), but because they at least mention the good links which are now buried at the bottom of the page. Also, they've been rate limiting ordinary users like crazy: more often than I'd like to, Google and DuckDuckGo now either show me either an error message or tell me to wait a bit. Sometimes I waste at least a minute refreshing the page and waiting for a result.\r\n\r\nI also can't browse GitHub freely without being logged in. Anubis is now everywhere, occasionally blocking me. I feel like my productivity has actually gone down thanks to all of that.\r\n\r\n_Edit: I see that most of the replies here are suggesting relying less on the web and going after more fundamental resources, which are great suggestions, don’t get me wrong! But a book from 5 years ago will not help you understand why your custom firmware doesn’t boot in your 1-year old mobile chipset. A existing bug report on the web probably will. Not quite my predicament at the moment, but something very similar for which the web seemed to work just fine before the AI craze._","submitter_user":"tmcb","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ask","web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ipplhe","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/ipplhe/how_are_you_dealing_with_all_negative_side"},{"short_id":"umsitb","created_at":"2026-11-08T11:07:51.827-06:00","title":"What can I realistically do with local AI on a Mac mini M5 Pro (48GB)?","url":"","score":4,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"\u003cp\u003eI just got a Mac mini with an M5 Pro (base CPU config) and 48GB of unified memory. I want to run as much AI locally as makes sense, but it's hard to separate what actually works day to day from benchmark hype.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat I'd like to do:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eCoding: autocomplete and agentic workflows with local models\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eImage generation\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eAnything else genuinely useful: transcription, RAG over my notes, etc.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eQuestions:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhich models and quantizations fit comfortably in 48GB and are actually usable, not just \"it runs\"?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhich runtime and tools: MLX, llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio, ComfyUI, something else?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhat's your setup, and what did you give up on and go back to cloud APIs for?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eReal tokens/sec numbers and \"this was a waste of time\" stories are especially welcome.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"I just got a Mac mini with an M5 Pro (base CPU config) and 48GB of unified memory. I want to run as much AI locally as makes sense, but it's hard to separate what actually works day to day from benchmark hype.\r\n\r\nWhat I'd like to do:\r\n\r\n- Coding: autocomplete and agentic workflows with local models\r\n- Image generation\r\n- Anything else genuinely useful: transcription, RAG over my notes, etc.\r\n\r\nQuestions:\r\n\r\n1. Which models and quantizations fit comfortably in 48GB and are actually usable, not just \"it runs\"?\r\n2. Which runtime and tools: MLX, llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio, ComfyUI, something else?\r\n3. What's your setup, and what did you give up on and go back to cloud APIs for?\r\n\r\nReal tokens/sec numbers and \"this was a waste of time\" stories are especially welcome.","submitter_user":"napolux","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ai","ask","mac"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/umsitb","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/umsitb/what_can_i_realistically_do_with_local_ai"},{"short_id":"lhovnm","created_at":"2026-11-07T17:31:05.716-06:00","title":"Dear Open Source Developers: It's OK not to embargo","url":"https://opensourcesecurity.io/2026/10-embargo/","score":27,"flags":0,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"liberty","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lhovnm","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lhovnm/dear_open_source_developers_it_s_ok_not"},{"short_id":"j63dme","created_at":"2026-11-07T03:21:55.256-06:00","title":"Meridian — an experimental web browser built on Servo and GPUI","url":"https://op.tngl.io/meridian/","score":57,"flags":0,"comment_count":11,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"icy","user_is_author":false,"tags":["browsers","rust"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/j63dme","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/j63dme/meridian_experimental_web_browser_built"},{"short_id":"urg4yu","created_at":"2026-11-08T10:58:03.605-06:00","title":"Unattended reboots with encrypted disks","url":"https://jmmv.dev/2026/11/zkeys-intro.html","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"jmmv","user_is_author":true,"tags":["freebsd","security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/urg4yu","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/urg4yu/unattended_reboots_with_encrypted_disks"},{"short_id":"owsnms","created_at":"2026-11-07T15:05:35.812-06:00","title":"*sshole, or when are we finally going to get rid of C?","url":"https://sunshowers.io/posts/sshole/","score":48,"flags":4,"comment_count":74,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"novedevo","user_is_author":false,"tags":["rant"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/owsnms","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/owsnms/sshole_when_are_we_fin